\begin{block}{Scaling Regression Models}
\protect\phantomsection\label{sec:regression}
\textbf{Detection time model}:
\(T_{detect} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \cdot n + \beta_2 \cdot n^2\)

\textbf{Table: Detection time regression coefficients.}
\{\#tab:detection-regression\}

{\def\LTcaptype{none} % do not increment counter
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}lllll@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
Coefficient & Estimate & SE & 95\% CI & \(p\) \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\(\beta_0\) (intercept) & 8.2 & 1.1 & {[}6.0, 10.4{]} &
\$\textless\$0.0001 \\
\(\beta_1\) (linear) & 1.8 & 0.3 & {[}1.2, 2.4{]} &
\$\textless\$0.0001 \\
\(\beta_2\) (quadratic) & 0.012 & 0.003 & {[}0.006, 0.018{]} &
\$\textless\$0.0001 \\
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\end{longtable}
}

\(R^2 = 0.994\), indicating excellent fit. The dominant linear term
(\(\beta_1 = 1.8\)) confirms approximately linear scaling up to 50
agents, with the quadratic contribution (\(\beta_2 = 0.012\)) becoming
material only beyond this range.

\textbf{Memory model}:
\(M = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \cdot n + \gamma_2 \cdot n^2\)

\textbf{Table: Memory usage regression coefficients.}
\{\#tab:memory-regression\}

{\def\LTcaptype{none} % do not increment counter
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}lllll@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
Coefficient & Estimate & SE & 95\% CI & \(p\) \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\(\gamma_0\) (intercept) & 78.3 & 5.6 & {[}67.1, 89.5{]} &
\$\textless\$0.0001 \\
\(\gamma_1\) (linear) & 12.4 & 1.2 & {[}10.0, 14.8{]} &
\$\textless\$0.0001 \\
\(\gamma_2\) (quadratic) & 0.089 & 0.012 & {[}0.065, 0.113{]} &
\$\textless\$0.0001 \\
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\end{longtable}
}

Memory growth is quadratic, primarily due to trust matrix storage
(\(O(n^2)\)). The intercept (\(\gamma_0 \approx 78\) MB) reflects
baseline framework overhead independent of agent count.
\end{block}

